      "text": "After the first bounce, it rises to $\\frac{2}{3} \\times 243 = 162$ cm.\nAfter the second bounce, it rises to $\\frac{2}{3} \\times 162 = 108$ cm.\nAfter the third bounce, it rises to $\\frac{2}{3} \\times 108 = 72$ cm.\nAfter the fourth bounce, it rises to $\\frac{2}{3} \\times 72 = 48$ cm.\nAfter the fifth bounce, it rises to $\\frac{2}{3} \\times 48 = 32$ cm.\nTherefore, the ball first rises less than $30$ cm after $\\boxed{4}$ bounces.\nTo find the number of bounces needed for the ball to rise less than $30$ cm, we need to iterate through the sequence of heights it reaches with each bounce until the height is below $30$ cm.\n\nWe start with a height of $243$ cm and apply the factor $\\frac{2}{3}$:\n\n- After the first bounce, the height is $h_1 = 243 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 162$ cm.\n- After the second bounce, the height is $h_2 = 162 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 108$ cm.\n- After the third bounce, the height is $h_3 = 108 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 72$ cm.\n- After the fourth bounce, the height is $h_4 = 72 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 48$ cm.\n- After the fifth bounce, the height is $h_5 = 48 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 32$ cm.\n\nSince $h_5 = 32$ cm is less than $30$ cm, the ball first rises less than $30$ cm after $\\boxed{4}$ bounces.",
